## The idea

We want the longest run of cells that contains at most `material` ocean cells (each ocean cell can be filled in). That is a **sliding window**: extend the right edge one cell at a time; whenever the window holds too many ocean cells, move the left edge forward until it is affordable again. The longest window seen is the answer. Every follow-up keeps this shape: walk the array twice for the circular version, restart past obstacles, and count cost instead of cells for the weighted version.

## Step by step

1. left = 0, ocean = 0, best = 0.
2. For each right: if cells[right] is ocean, ocean++.
3. While ocean > material: if cells[left] is ocean, ocean--; left++.
4. The window [left, right] is now affordable: best = max(best, right − left + 1).
5. Circular: if all ocean fits, the answer is n. Otherwise run right from 0 to 2n−1 using index right % n, and also shrink when the window grows longer than n.
6. Obstacles (−1): when right hits an obstacle, start a fresh window just after it.
7. Weighted: add cost[right] instead of 1, and shrink while the total cost > budget.

## Worked example

**Input:** cells = [1,0,0,1,1,0,1], material = 1

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| right 0..1 | window [1,0] → 1 ocean → best 2 |
| right 2 | [1,0,0] → 2 ocean > 1 → shrink: drop 1, drop 0 → window [0] (index 2), ocean 1 |
| right 3..4 | window [0,1,1] → best 3 |
| right 5 | adds ocean → 2 > 1 → shrink past index 2 → window [1,1,0] (3..5) |
| right 6 | window [1,1,0,1] (3..6) → length 4 → best 4 |

**Result:** 4

## Cost

O(n) time — each pointer moves at most n (2n in the circular version) steps. O(1) extra memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

